  "query_preview": "..The upper low that brought windy conditions to our\narea on Tuesday continues to lift into the northern Rockies. A\nsignificant plume of deep monsoon moisture continues to flow \nnorthward into New Mexico, with widespread rain and thunderstorms.\nOver northern Arizona, drier air remains for much of\nCoconino/Yavapai counties early this morning, with dewpoints in\nthe 30s/40s. Farther east, dewpoints have risen into the 50s. The\nair mass is forecast to moisten from east to west through the day,\nwith scattered thunderstorms forecast to develop along the higher\nterrain of the Mogollon Rim, White Mtns, and Yavapai County by\nearly afternoon. Isolated showers and thunderstorms will be\npossible elsewhere. Storms are forecast to drift south through the\nlower elevations of Yavapai/Gila counties into the late\nafternoon/early evening hours. Some localized heavy rainfall/flash\nflooding will be possible, though storm intensity may not be too\nextreme as CAPE values are generally forecast to remain <1000 J/kg\nper the latest HREF. On Thursday, elevated precipitable water\nvalues remain over the area, along with a disturbance moving in\nfrom the south providing lift. Another round of scattered showers\nand thunderstorms is expected during the afternoon and evening. \nAgain, the primary threat with these will be locally heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding. By Friday, the upper level \ndisturbances will be east of our area, with some drying from west \nto east. There is still a slight chance of some afternoon storms, \nmainly near the AZ/NM state line with a 10% chance as far west as \nFlagstaff.\n\nMajority of the guidance indicates drier conditions Saturday and\nmost of Sunday. By later Sunday into Monday, we'll need to \nmonitor the possibility of deeper moisture getting pulled\nnorthward into a developing west coast trough, originating from a\ncouple of tropical systems off the Baja coast. For now, PoP \nvalues move back upward to 20-30% late Sunday and 30-60% for\nMonday. There is still quite a bit of uncertainty in the details\nof the interaction between the trough and the tropical moisture\nduring this period, with a few of the ensemble solutions showing a\nthreat of heavy rainfall. This will be something to monitor over \nthe next few days.",
